  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "$S=\\{A,B,C,D\\}$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write down all the subsets of $S$, then explain why there are exactly $2^4$ of them.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Subsets: $\\varnothing$; $\\{A\\},\\{B\\},\\{C\\},\\{D\\}$; $\\{A,B\\},\\{A,C\\},\\{A,D\\},\\{B,C\\},\\{B,D\\},\\{C,D\\}$; $\\{A,B,C\\},\\{A,B,D\\},\\{A,C,D\\},\\{B,C,D\\}$; $\\{A,B,C,D\\}$. Each of the four elements is included or excluded independently, giving $2^4=16$ subsets.",
          "worked": "0-letter subsets: $\\varnothing$.\n\n1-letter subsets: $\\{A\\},\\{B\\},\\{C\\},\\{D\\}$.\n\n2-letter subsets: $\\{A,B\\},\\{A,C\\},\\{A,D\\},\\{B,C\\},\\{B,D\\},\\{C,D\\}$.\n\n3-letter subsets: $\\{A,B,C\\},\\{A,B,D\\},\\{A,C,D\\},\\{B,C,D\\}$.\n\n4-letter subsets: $\\{A,B,C,D\\}$.\n\nTotal: $1+4+6+4+1=16=2^4$.\n\nFor each element there are two choices: include it or exclude it. The four choices are independent, so the number of subsets is $2\\times2\\times2\\times2=2^4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence explain why $\\binom40+\\binom41+\\binom42+\\binom43+\\binom44=2^4$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom4r$ counts the $r$-element subsets. Summing for $r=0,1,2,3,4$ counts all $2^4$ subsets exactly once.",
          "worked": "$\\binom40$ counts the 0-letter subsets: 1 subset.\n\n$\\binom41$ counts the 1-letter subsets: 4 subsets.\n\n$\\binom42$ counts the 2-letter subsets: 6 subsets.\n\n$\\binom43$ counts the 3-letter subsets: 4 subsets.\n\n$\\binom44$ counts the 4-letter subsets: 1 subset.\n\nThese mutually exclusive cases include every subset, so\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\binom40+\\binom41+\\binom42+\\binom43+\\binom44&=1+4+6+4+1\\\\&=16\\\\&=2^4.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Explain why $\\binom n0+\\binom n1+\\cdots+\\binom nn=2^n$, for any whole number $n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "An $n$-element set has $2^n$ subsets because each element is included or excluded independently. There are $\\binom nr$ subsets of size $r$, so summing over all sizes $0$ to $n$ gives $2^n$.",
          "worked": "For an $n$-element set, each element has two choices: include it or exclude it. Independent choices give $2^n$ subsets in total.\n\n$\\binom n0$ counts subsets with 0 elements, $\\binom n1$ counts subsets with 1 element, and so on, up to $\\binom nn$, which counts subsets with $n$ elements.\n\nEvery subset has exactly one of these sizes, so counting by size gives\n$$\\binom n0+\\binom n1+\\cdots+\\binom nn=2^n.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
